    "events_list#doc GET /api/projects/:project_id/events": "The example is rendered from the schema with an autocaptured element in elements; elements are what PostHog derives from posthog-js autocapture ($elements), which no demanded application sends (Rallly turns autocapture off, packages/posthog/src/client.ts) and the twin does not derive. The life and the capture examples read events back through this list. Source: api/spec/openapi.json.gz, ClickhouseEvent.elements.",
    "persons_list#doc GET /api/projects/:project_id/persons": "The example is rendered from the reference schema (id 0, a number; properties null); the server's persons list answers the serialized actor, whose id is the person's UUID (spec/patches.json, PersonListRecord.id, from posthog/api/person.py). The life and the identify examples read persons back through this list. Source: api/spec/openapi.json.gz, PersonListRecord; api/spec/patches.json.",
    "evaluateFlags#doc Flags page request with configuration": "The page's answer is the flags service's fallback configuration, which it answers only where the project has no cached remote config: config, toolbarParams and isAuthenticated are fields of ConfigResponse::fallback alone (rust/feature-flags/src/api/types.rs, 'Create a minimal fallback config for cache miss/error scenarios.'), and a project's own config, built by RemoteConfig.build_config (posthog/models/remote_config.py), carries none of them. The World holds the project's remote config, so the flags service passes that through (handler/config_response_builder.rs, 'Passed through cached config from HyperCache'); the life's act 5 asserts it beside /array/<token>/config's same fields."
  }
